        "reflection": "Proverbs 31 has done more damage to mothers than almost any other passage — not because of what it says, but because of what people have done with it. Read carefully, the woman here isn't an impossible performance standard. She's a picture of character, not a checklist of tasks. And the verses that close the portrait are the key: strength and dignity are her clothing, and she laughs at the time to come. I don't want you to miss what gives her that laughter. It's not that she has everything under control. It's that her fear is settled — her confidence is in the Lord, not in her ability to manage outcomes. The passage says it plainly: charm is deceptive and beauty is fleeting, but a woman who fears the Lord is to be praised. That's the exact opposite of what culture tells mothers — that your worth is earned through performance, appearance, and how your kids turn out. Her strength was never efficiency. It's a settled identity in God that makes her unafraid of the future. And that's available to any mother willing to put her worth in the right place — not in being impressive, but in being his.",
