        "reflection": "Paul writes to Timothy and does something remarkable — he names the women who gave Timothy his faith before he names himself. Lois and Eunice, his grandmother and mother, are credited with planting a sincere faith in this young man who'd go on to shake cities. Paul calls it sincere — not a performed faith, not an inherited religion, but the genuine article, real enough to hold when everything else fell apart. And notice Paul doesn't describe what Lois and Eunice did to pull it off. He just names the result. We can assume it was exactly what Deuteronomy 6 describes — faith lived in the ordinary, passed through relationship, made believable by consistency over years. I don't want you to miss what this says to you as a mother: the faith you carry has a trajectory that runs far beyond you. The sincere faith you fight for and live in front of your children is a seed with a harvest you may never fully see. That's both a weight and a hope. A weight, because sincerity matters — you can't fake it at close range, and your kids are at close range. A hope, because God uses faithful mothers in ways they never get to witness.",
