        "reflection": "Romans 8 is Paul's biggest statement about life in the Spirit, and verse 26 lands right in the middle of it like a lifeline for the exhausted: the Spirit helps us in our weakness, because we don't know how to pray as we ought, but the Spirit himself intercedes for us with groanings too deep for words. I don't want you to miss who Paul is writing to — people who already know how to pray. And he's telling them there are moments when words fail and the need is bigger than language, and in those moments, the Spirit takes over. This isn't a verse about spiritual failure. It's a verse about the God who closes the gap between what you can bring and what's actually needed. Every mother knows the prayer with no words — the 3 a.m. by a sick child, the tears in the car after a conversation went wrong, the fear with no name attached. In those moments you're not failing to pray well. You're standing exactly where this verse lives. The Spirit is interceding for you according to the will of God — which means the prayer you can't even form is still being offered, and offered perfectly. Your weakness was never the last word. It's the starting point.",
