        "reflection": "Psalm 46 was written for crisis — real, earth-shaking crisis. The imagery is mountains hurled into the sea, nations raging, kingdoms tottering. The psalmist isn't describing a manageable inconvenience. He's describing the world coming apart. And the refrain isn't a question, it's a statement: God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. I don't want you to miss that phrase, very present — in the Hebrew it carries the sense of nearness. Not just that God exists and is theoretically available, but that he's right here, close in, accessible in the exact place the chaos is. Now, the chaos of motherhood rarely looks like mountains falling into the sea. It looks like a toddler's meltdown and a deadline and no sleep and a strained marriage and a question you don't know how to answer. But the emotional reality is the same: the sense that things are beyond you. And what this psalm offers isn't the removal of the chaos. It's the presence of God in the middle of it. Be still and know that I am God isn't a command to feel peaceful — it's a command to stop striving long enough to notice who is actually in the room with you.",
